IE520-sa u3: `vlan-stacking ?` (global) → `% Unrecognized command`; `switchport vlan-stacking ?` →
customer-edge-port | provider-port; `switchport vlan ?` → translation.
AW+ wiki: `vlan-stacking vlan outer-vlan` (Global Configuration, "Version 5.5.0-1.1: command
added") is documented for AR4050/x230/x908Gen2/x930/x950. It is not on this IE520 build.
⇒ **The feature the case tests (VLAN-based QinQ) is absent. UNSUPPORTED.**

## Supplementary — double tagging that IS offered, with translation on the same port (not graded)
Topology: tb470 eth1 ↔ stack port3.0.13 (customer side) · stack port4.0.26 ↔ SA port1.0.26 (SX
link, provider side) · SA port1.0.2 ↔ tb470 eth2 (observer). eth3 (x230) is the control capture.
Scratch VLANs 3995 (S-VLAN) and 3996 (wire VID), both deleted afterwards. Customer tag 2100. Sender:
vsend.py 100 × IPv4/UDP, unknown-unicast dst 00:00:5e:00:53:ff, marker CKVLAN-<stage>. Captures:
tcpdump -w filtered on the sender MAC, counted by vcount.py.

Config (12:51–12:53, ckcon --stop-on-error; the stack end shut during config; no `% ` line except
two invalid SHOW forms of mine, `show interface <p> switchport`, after the config had applied):
```
stack: vlan 3995 name ck-svlan / vlan 3996 name ck-wire
  port3.0.13: switchport access vlan 3995 / switchport vlan-stacking customer-edge-port
  port4.0.26: switchport mode trunk / switchport trunk allowed vlan add 3995 / switchport vlan-stacking provider-port
              / switchport trunk native vlan 4000   (added before no-shut: without it the trunk's native VLAN 1
                would have re-created the VLAN-1 loop with sa3)
SA:    vlan 3995, 3996; port1.0.26 + port1.0.2: switchport mode trunk / trunk allowed vlan add 3995,3996;
       port1.0.26 trunk native vlan 4000
```
Read back: `show vlan 3995` → port3.0.13(u) port4.0.26(t); running-config per above. Links
connected a-full a-1000; loop-protection on 4.0.26 per-VLAN (vlan3995, vlan4000) Normal; SA
1.0.2/1.0.26 Normal.

| stage | stack config under test | direction | sent | captured | result |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| s1f | port-based QinQ only | eth1 C-tag 2100 → eth2 | 100 | eth2 100 × `tags=3995,2100`; eth3 0 | outer pushed, customer tag kept |
| s1r | port-based QinQ only | eth2 3995,2100 → eth1 | 100 | eth1 100 × `tags=2100`; eth3 0 | outer popped at the customer edge |
| s2f | + `switchport vlan translation vlan 3996 vlan 3995` on provider port4.0.26 (ACCEPTED alongside `vlan-stacking provider-port`) | eth1 2100 → eth2 | 100 | eth2 100 × `tags=3996,2100`; eth3 0 | **translation applied to the OUTER tag**, inner untouched |
| s2r | same | eth2 3996,2100 → eth1 | 100 | eth1 100 × `tags=2100` | wire 3996 → internal 3995, popped |
| s2r5 | same | eth2 3995,2100 → eth1 | 100 | eth1 100 × `tags=2100` | untranslated VID passes (`default accept`) |
| s3f | port3.0.13 → trunk + `switchport vlan translation vlan 2100 outer-vlan 3995`; port4.0.26 plain trunk + translation 3996↔3995 | eth1 2100 → eth2 | 100 | eth2 100 × `tags=3996` (single tag) | **customer tag 2100 LOST** (O-2) |
| s3r | same | eth2 3996,2100 → eth1 | 100 | eth1 100 × `tags=2100` | pops correctly |
| s4f | s3 + `switchport vlan-stacking provider-port` back on port4.0.26 | eth1 2100 → eth2 | 100 | eth2 100 × `tags=3996` | inner still lost |
| s4r | same | eth2 3996,2100 → eth1 | 100 | eth1 100 × `tags=2100` | pops correctly |

`show interface switchport vlan translation` (s3/s4): port3.0.13 wire 2100 / VLAN 0 / Outer 3995,
default accept; port4.0.26 wire 3996 / VLAN 3995 / Outer 0, default accept. DUT counters (s1f):
port3.0.13 input +100 (2142 → 2242). Raw: evidence/27887/27887-s*.out and .pcap.

**O-2 (observation, not graded):** on the IE520, the per-VLAN double-tag translation entry
`switchport vlan translation vlan <wire> outer-vlan <outer>` forwards customer frames with ONE tag
(the outer VID, translated on egress). The customer tag is dropped, both through a plain trunk and
through a provider-port egress. The AW+ x930 page describes this entry as adding "the outer-tag VID
to be added to packets received on the port" (a push). The reverse direction pops correctly. The
same double tagging done with port-based `switchport vlan-stacking` keeps the inner tag (s1f/s2f).
Possible defect or platform difference. Terrence's call.

